lesson physics-deeper-water-pushes-harder v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=2 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Deeper water pushes harder"
  goal "Use the depth of water to work out how hard it pushes, and explain why a lock gate is built strongest at the bottom."

  look {
    scene 27x32 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        spouts t depth=40 holes=[10, 20, 30] show=1
        canallock l level=0.5 boat=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Water pushes on everything it touches, and the deeper it is, the harder it pushes. Make three
      holes in a tank and the jet from the lowest hole comes out fastest, because it has the most
      water above it; with the tank up on its stand, as here, that jet also lands furthest. Twice as
      deep pushes twice as hard. In the canal, water pushes 10 kilonewtons (10 kN) on every square
      metre for each metre of depth, so a lock's gates are built thickest at the bottom. How much
      water there is does not matter, only how deep it is.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.spouts-furthest count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.lock-gates count=1 seed=1
    level easy {
      worked physics.pressure-depth q=0 d=4 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.pressure-depth count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=4
    practice physics.spouts-depth count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=3 hard-count=1
    practice physics.spouts-furthest count=1 seed=5 easy-seed=9 hard-seed=7
    level hard {
      show physics.spouts-depth v=2
      show physics.pressure-depth q=1 d=2.5
    }
  }
  story {
    show physics.lock-difference u=3.5 c=1.5 easy-u=3 easy-c=2 hard-u=4 hard-c=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.spouts-same-depth v=0 easy-v=1 hard-v=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.lock-fill-limit v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "The deeper the water, the harder it pushes: 10 kN on each square metre for every metre of depth."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The common wrong idea is that more water pushes harder, so a wide tank or a big lake would push
    harder than a narrow one. It is the depth alone that counts, and the two-star question puts a
    narrow tank beside a wide one to test exactly that. Listen for "more water" in the child's
    reasons and ask "how deep is the hole?" instead. The numbers are real: at the bottom of a lock
    3 m deep the water pushes 30 kN, about the weight of three tonnes, on every square metre of the
    gate. If the kilonewtons get in the way, go back to the tank and the jets, which show the same
    rule without numbers, then stay here another day. Off the page, pierce three holes one above the
    other in a plastic bottle, fill it in the sink, and watch which jet goes furthest. The three-star
    question is worked backwards from a limit and then forwards at a rate.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the deepest hole is lettered C, a worked example turns a depth into a push, the depths are whole metres going to a push, the lock's two sides are a metre apart, and the three-star lock starts at 1 m and fills 50 cm a minute."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the holes are lettered in a shuffled order, the pushes are worked back to depths, a second depth question is asked, the lock's sides are three metres apart, and the three-star lock starts at 1.2 m and fills 20 cm a minute."
  }
}
